SPECIAL ACT ON STRENGTHENING THE COMPETITIVENESS OF AND SUPPORTING THE SEMICONDUCTOR INDUSTRY

CHAPTER I GENERAL PROVISIONS

Article 1 (Purpose)
The purpose of this Act is to contribute to national and economic security and the development of the national economy by establishing a foundation for sustainable growth, including the creation of an innovation ecosystem for the semiconductor industry, thereby strengthening the competitiveness of the semiconductor industry of the Republic of Korea and establishing semiconductor sovereignty.

Article 2 (Definitions)
The terms used in this Act are defined as follows:
1. The term "semiconductor industry" means an industry falling under any of the following items:
(a) An industry that designs or manufactures memory semiconductors, non-memory semiconductors (meaning system semiconductors, optical discrete devices, and other items publicly notified by the Minister of Trade and Industry), etc.;
(b) An industry that manufactures and supplies semiconductors under contract based on semiconductor design intellectual property designed by outside enterprises;
(c) An industry that supplies products and services forming the supply chain ecosystem, including design, design intellectual property, software, materials, components, equipment, and packaging, necessary for the industries specified in items (a) and (b);
(d) An industry that conducts research, development, etc. necessary for the industries specified in items (a) through (c).
2. The term "semiconductor cluster" means an area designated under Article 11 from among the following areas where relevant institutions and facilities are concentrated to promote domestic and foreign investment, technology development, and design, manufacturing, supply, research, sales, support, etc., with a view to creating an innovation ecosystem to strengthen the competitiveness of the semiconductor industry:
(a) An industrial complex under subparagraph 8 of Article 2 of the Industrial Sites and Development Act;
(b) A specialized complex for national high-tech strategic industries under subparagraph 3 of Article 2 of the Act on Special Measures for Strengthening and Protecting the Competitiveness of National High-Tech Strategic Industries;
(c) Other areas prescribed by Presidential Decree as being recognized as requiring designation as semiconductor clusters.

Article 3 (Responsibilities of State and Local Governments, etc.)
(1) The State and local governments shall recognize the importance of the semiconductor industry to the State and the national economy and devise policies necessary to strengthen the competitiveness of the semiconductor industry.
(2) The State and local governments shall endeavor to secure stable financial resources necessary to establish a foundation for sustainable growth, including the creation of an innovation ecosystem to strengthen the competitiveness of the semiconductor industry.
(3) Business operators engaged in the semiconductor industry shall endeavor to contribute to strengthening the competitiveness of the semiconductor industry and to industrial development through the development of innovative semiconductor industry technologies, manufacturing, etc.

Article 4 (Relationship to Other Acts)
This Act shall take precedence over other Acts with respect to strengthening the competitiveness of the semiconductor industry, providing support, easing regulations, etc.: Provided, That where applying another Act is more favorable to business operators engaged in the semiconductor industry and to the development of the semiconductor industry, that Act shall apply.

CHAPTER II FORMULATION OF MASTER PLAN FOR STRENGTHENING SEMICONDUCTOR INDUSTRY COMPETITIVENESS, ETC.

Article 5 (Formulation of Master Plan for Strengthening Semiconductor Industry Competitiveness, etc.)
(1) The Government shall formulate a master plan for strengthening semiconductor industry competitiveness (hereinafter referred to as the "master plan") in five-year units to strengthen the competitiveness of the semiconductor industry.
(2) The master plan shall include the following matters:
1. The basic direction for strengthening the competitiveness of the semiconductor industry;
2. Matters concerning the installation and expansion of industrial infrastructure, including electricity grids, water supply networks, and road networks, for the development of the semiconductor industry;
3. Matters concerning trends and development prospects in the semiconductor industry;
4. Matters concerning the establishment and improvement of systems for strengthening the competitiveness of the semiconductor industry;
5. Matters concerning the creation of an innovation ecosystem for the semiconductor industry;
6. Matters concerning the smooth localization of semiconductor materials, components, and equipment and the securing of stable supply chains;
7. Matters concerning expedited authorizations and permits for investment in facilities or research and development relating to semiconductor materials, components, and equipment and the semiconductor industry;
8. Matters concerning the training and protection of specialized personnel in the semiconductor industry;
9. Matters concerning support for research and development in the semiconductor industry;
10. Matters concerning financial and administrative support for strengthening semiconductor industry competitiveness;
11. Matters concerning raising and managing financial resources necessary to strengthen semiconductor industry competitiveness;
12. Other matters related to strengthening the competitiveness of the semiconductor industry that are prescribed by Presidential Decree.
(3) The Minister of Trade and Industry shall formulate the master plan by consolidating sectoral plans of the relevant central administrative agencies and finalize it following deliberation by the Special Committee for Strengthening Semiconductor Industry Competitiveness under Article 9. The same shall apply to amendments to the master plan (excluding amendments to minor matters prescribed by Presidential Decree).
(4) When the Minister of Trade and Industry finalizes the master plan under paragraph (3), the Minister shall report it without delay to the competent standing committee of the National Assembly.
(5) Where deemed necessary to formulate the master plan, the Minister of Trade and Industry may request the heads of relevant central administrative agencies to submit materials necessary for its formulation. In such cases, the heads of relevant central administrative agencies shall comply with the request unless there is a special reason not to do so.
(6) Other matters necessary for the formulation and implementation of the master plan shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.

Article 6 (Formulation of Action Plans for Strengthening Semiconductor Industry Competitiveness)
(1) The heads of relevant central administrative agencies shall formulate and implement annual action plans for strengthening semiconductor industry competitiveness (hereinafter referred to as "action plans") in accordance with the master plan.
(2) The heads of relevant central administrative agencies shall annually submit the results of implementation of the preceding year's action plans and the following year's action plans to the Special Committee for Strengthening Semiconductor Industry Competitiveness under Article 9.
(3) Other matters necessary for the formulation and implementation of action plans shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.

Article 7 (Surveys of Current State of Semiconductor Industry and Forecasts of Development Prospects)
(1) The Government shall annually survey the current economic, industrial, and international state of the semiconductor industry, etc. and forecast its development prospects, and shall reflect the results in the formulation of the master plan and action plans.
(2) Matters necessary for surveys of the current state and forecasts of development prospects under paragraph (1) shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.

Article 8 (Compilation of Statistics concerning Semiconductor Industry)
(1) The Government shall compile and manage statistics concerning the semiconductor industry to establish a foundation for strengthening the competitiveness of the semiconductor industry and to systematically formulate the master plan and action plans.
(2) The Statistics Act shall apply mutatis mutandis to the compilation of statistics under paragraph (1).
(3) The Government may have a relevant specialized institution perform the duties of compiling and managing statistics under paragraph (1), as prescribed by Presidential Decree. In such cases, the Government may provide support for all or part of the costs of performing such duties within budgetary limits.
(4) Other matters necessary for compiling and managing statistics under paragraph (1) shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.

Article 9 (Special Committee for Strengthening Semiconductor Industry Competitiveness)
(1) The Special Committee for Strengthening Semiconductor Industry Competitiveness (hereinafter referred to as the "Committee") shall be established under the President to deliberate and decide on the following matters concerning major policies and plans related to strengthening the competitiveness of the semiconductor industry:
1. Matters concerning the formulation and implementation of the master plan and action plans, and the examination and evaluation of implementation results;
2. Matters concerning the installation and expansion of industrial infrastructure, including electricity grids, water supply networks, and road networks, for the development of the semiconductor industry;
3. Matters concerning regulatory improvements necessary to strengthen the competitiveness of the semiconductor industry, including the unification of regulations of relevant central administrative agencies and the expansion of provisions deeming authorizations and permits granted;
4. Matters concerning the creation of and support for an innovation ecosystem for the semiconductor industry for the design, manufacturing, supply, etc. of semiconductors;
5. Matters concerning the simplification of procedures, recommendations for improving relevant statutes and regulations, provision of special exceptions, and resolution of difficulties to strengthen the competitiveness of the semiconductor industry;
6. Matters concerning the development, operation, designation, revocation of designation, and support of semiconductor clusters;
7. Matters concerning the deemed granting of authorizations, permits, etc. and special provisions for expedited processing for project implementers of semiconductor clusters;
8. Matters concerning the deemed granting of authorizations, permits, etc. and special provisions for expedited processing for the installation of industrial infrastructure in semiconductor clusters;
9. Matters concerning support for enterprises and institutions located in semiconductor clusters;
10. Matters concerning special provisions for semiconductor industry support by relevant central administrative agencies and local governments;
11. Other matters deemed necessary by the chairperson in connection with strengthening semiconductor industry competitiveness, including financial and administrative support.
(2) The Committee shall consist of not more than 20 members, including one chairperson. The President shall serve as chairperson, and its members shall be the following persons:
1. The heads of relevant central administrative agencies prescribed by Presidential Decree;
2. Persons commissioned by the chairperson from among persons engaged in business, industry, academia, research institutions, etc. who have extensive expertise and experience in strengthening semiconductor industry competitiveness.
(3) The Committee shall have one executive secretary member, who shall be the Minister of Trade and Industry.
(4) Where necessary to perform the work of the Committee in a specialized manner, specialized committees for individual fields composed of members of the Committee or outside experts may be established. Where necessary to discuss particular current issues related to policies for strengthening the competitiveness of the semiconductor industry, the following special committees composed of members of the Committee or outside experts may be established:
1. A special committee for responding to semiconductor industry crises;
2. Other special committees deemed necessary by the chairperson.
(5) The Committee shall have a Semiconductor Industry Coordination Committee (hereinafter referred to as the "Coordination Committee") to conduct advance reviews, coordination, and consultations on matters to be deliberated and decided by the Committee, and to address matters delegated by the Committee in accordance with criteria prescribed by Presidential Decree. The Vice Minister of Trade and Industry shall chair the Coordination Committee.
(6) Subcommittees may be established to facilitate the efficient conduct of deliberations by the Coordination Committee.
(7) Where necessary to perform its duties under paragraph (1), the Committee may hear the opinions of relevant experts with specialized knowledge and experience or request cooperation, including the submission of materials or opinions, from relevant central administrative agencies and local governments, public institutions, research institutions, business operators, or other institutions or organizations.
(8) Except as provided in paragraphs (1) through (6), matters necessary for the composition and operation of the Committee, specialized committees, the Coordination Committee, subcommittees, etc. shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.

Article 10 (Semiconductor Innovative Growth Support Unit)
(1) A Semiconductor Innovative Growth Support Unit (hereinafter referred to as the "Support Unit") shall be established in the Ministry of Trade and Industry to support the operation of the Committee and efficiently perform the duties under Articles 5, 6, 9, 36, etc.
(2) Where necessary to support the Support Unit's work on strengthening semiconductor industry competitiveness and promoting innovative growth, the Minister of Trade and Industry may request the secondment or concurrent assignment of public officials of relevant central administrative agencies and officers and employees of public institutions, research institutions, and relevant institutions, organizations, research institutes, etc., and may employ experts in relevant fields as public officials with fixed terms within budgetary limits.
(3) Other matters necessary for the composition and operation of the Support Unit shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.

CHAPTER III DESIGNATION OF AND SUPPORT FOR SEMICONDUCTOR CLUSTERS

Article 11 (Designation of Semiconductor Clusters)
(1) The Minister of Trade and Industry may designate semiconductor clusters to establish an innovation ecosystem for the semiconductor industry and thereby promote domestic and foreign investment, technology development, and design, manufacturing, supply, research, sales, etc. In such cases, the Minister shall receive applications that include semiconductor cluster development plans, etc., or directly designate semiconductor clusters where necessary, in accordance with the criteria prescribed by Presidential Decree, following consultation with the heads of the relevant central administrative agencies and local governments.
(2) Where a semiconductor cluster is designated and developed under paragraph (1) in an area referred to in items (a) and (b) of subparagraph 2 of Article 2, the Industrial Sites and Development Act, the Act on Special Cases concerning the Simplification of Authorization and Permission Procedures for Industrial Complexes, and the Industrial Cluster Development and Factory Establishment Act shall be complied with. In such cases, where the Minister of Trade and Industry deems that prompt development of a semiconductor cluster is necessary, the Minister may select the relevant area and request the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport to designate it as a national industrial complex under Article 6 (1) of the Industrial Sites and Development Act, and the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport receiving such a request shall endeavor to give priority to the designation of the national industrial complex.
(3) The heads of relevant central administrative agencies, the Special Metropolitan City Mayor, Metropolitan City Mayors, the Special Self-Governing City Mayor, Do Governors, or Special Self-Governing Province Governors (hereinafter referred to as "Mayors/Do Governors"), project implementers developing semiconductor clusters (hereinafter referred to as "project implementers"), etc. may apply to the Minister of Trade and Industry for designation of a semiconductor cluster under paragraph (1), attaching a semiconductor cluster development plan that includes matters prescribed by Presidential Decree, such as site development and directions for the development of the semiconductor cluster.
(4) A business operator engaged in the semiconductor industry who wishes to be designated as a project implementer for a semiconductor cluster under paragraph (3) may apply to the Minister of Trade and Industry in accordance with the criteria prescribed by Presidential Decree.
(5) Upon receiving an application for designation of a semiconductor cluster under paragraph (1), or where deemed necessary, the Minister of Trade and Industry may, following deliberation and resolution by the Committee, approve a semiconductor cluster development plan (including an amendment to an approval) and designate a semiconductor cluster in an area that meets all of the following requirements. In such cases, a semiconductor cluster may be designated so as to overlap with a specialized complex for national high-tech strategic industries under subparagraph 3 of Article 2 of the Act on Special Measures for Strengthening and Protecting the Competitiveness of National High-Tech Strategic Industries, a special opportunity development zone under subparagraph 13 of Article 2 of the Special Act on Local Decentralization and Balanced Growth, or a special research and development zone under subparagraph 1 of Article 2 of the Special Act on Promotion of Special Research and Development Zones. <Amended on June 2, 2026>
1. It shall be possible to secure demand for occupancy from domestic and foreign enterprises;
2. It shall be possible to secure the site and infrastructure necessary for the semiconductor cluster, including information and communications networks, water, and electricity;
3. The plan for raising the necessary financial resources shall be feasible;
4. The area shall meet other requirements prescribed by Presidential Decree for recognition as suitable for development into a semiconductor cluster.
(6) When designating a semiconductor cluster under paragraph (1), the Minister of Trade and Industry shall take balanced regional development into consideration.
(7) Other matters necessary for the approval of semiconductor cluster development plans, designation of semiconductor clusters and project implementers, provision of financial support and special exceptions, etc. shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.

Article 12 (Amendment or Cancellation of Designation of Semiconductor Clusters)
(1) The Minister of Trade and Industry may amend or cancel the designation of a semiconductor cluster in any of the following cases, following deliberation and resolution by the Committee: Provided, That where the Minister of Trade and Industry intends to amend minor matters prescribed by Presidential Decree, the Minister may make such amendments without deliberation by the Committee, following consultation with the heads of relevant central administrative agencies or the Mayors/Do Governors having jurisdiction over the semiconductor cluster concerned:
1. Where financial support under Article 14 or 15 has been used for purposes other than its original purpose;
2. Where achievement of the purpose of designating the semiconductor cluster is deemed impossible.
(2) Other matters necessary for the amendment or cancellation of designation of semiconductor clusters shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.

Article 13 (Measures to Promote Semiconductor Clusters)
(1) The Government shall formulate and implement measures to promote semiconductor clusters (hereinafter referred to as "promotion measures") for their innovative development and operation.
(2) Promotion measures shall include the following matters:
1. Basic directions for promoting semiconductor clusters;
2. Establishment of industrial infrastructure to promote semiconductor clusters;
3. Support for investment in equipment and research and development activities by enterprises and institutions located in semiconductor clusters;
4. Plans for raising financial resources for the systematic development and operation of semiconductor clusters;
5. Applications for intellectual property rights in research and development outcomes;
6. Other matters prescribed by Presidential Decree to promote semiconductor clusters.
(3) Before formulating or amending promotion measures, the Government shall consult with the heads of relevant central administrative agencies and the relevant Mayors/Do Governors.
(4) The Government shall formulate and implement measures concerning location, financial and administrative support, etc. to efficiently implement promotion measures.

Article 14 (Support for Development and Operation of Industrial Infrastructure in Semiconductor Clusters)
(1) The State and local governments shall promptly develop and support the following industrial infrastructure for the smooth development and operation of semiconductor clusters and shall give priority to providing support for all or part of the costs thereof in accordance with the criteria prescribed by Presidential Decree: <Amended on March 17, 2026>
1. Facilities prescribed by Presidential Decree for the supply of electricity (including renewable energy power generation under subparagraph 4 of Article 2 of the Act on the Promotion of the Development, Use and Diffusion of Renewable Energy and hydrogen power generation under subparagraph 7-3 of Article 2 of the Hydrogen Economy Promotion and Hydrogen Safety Management Act);
2. Facilities prescribed by Presidential Decree for the supply of water;
3. Facilities prescribed by Presidential Decree for the treatment of wastewater and waste;
4. Roads prescribed by Presidential Decree that connect to semiconductor clusters;
5. Other facilities prescribed by Presidential Decree for the smooth development and operation of semiconductor clusters.
(2) In connection with the development of industrial infrastructure under paragraph (1), the State and local governments shall consider the relevant schedules and plans for investment, operation, etc. of semiconductor cluster project implementers and enterprises (referring to enterprises prescribed by Presidential Decree, including enterprises that have concluded occupancy agreements; hereinafter the same shall apply) and institutions located in semiconductor clusters.
(3) A support council in which relevant central administrative agencies, local governments, enterprises and institutions located in semiconductor clusters, etc. participate may be established to support the development and operation of semiconductor clusters.
(4) Other matters necessary for support for the development and operation of semiconductor clusters shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.

Article 15 (Support for Enterprises and Institutions Located in Semiconductor Clusters, etc.)
(1) The State and local governments shall provide financial and administrative support, in accordance with the criteria prescribed by Presidential Decree, to business operators engaged in the semiconductor industry and enterprises and institutions located in semiconductor clusters to support investment in equipment and research facilities related to the semiconductor industry, research and development investment, industrial infrastructure investment, the development and operation of semiconductor clusters, etc.
(2) The State and local governments may give priority over other projects to supporting the costs necessary for any of the following projects undertaken by business operators engaged in the semiconductor industry or enterprises and institutions located in semiconductor clusters:
1. Installation and operation of industrial cluster infrastructure and industrial infrastructure under subparagraphs 9 and 10 of Article 2 of the Industrial Cluster Development and Factory Establishment Act for investment related to the semiconductor industry and semiconductor clusters;
2. Establishment of knowledge industry centers under subparagraph 13 of Article 2 of the Industrial Cluster Development and Factory Establishment Act;
3. Development of sites for investment related to the semiconductor industry and semiconductor clusters and installation of various convenience facilities, including medical facilities, educational facilities, and housing;
4. Investment in equipment by reshoring enterprises under subparagraph 4 of Article 2 of the Act on Assistance to Korean Off-Shore Enterprises in Repatriation;
5. Projects for developing technoparks under subparagraph 1 of Article 2 of the Act on Special Cases concerning Support of Technoparks;
6. Industrial technology development projects under Article 11 of the Industrial Technology Innovation Promotion Act;
7. Industrial technology infrastructure development projects under Article 19 of the Industrial Technology Innovation Promotion Act;
8. Projects of local governments to promote technology transfer and commercialization under Article 17 of the Technology Transfer and Commercialization Promotion Act;
9. Other projects determined and publicly notified by the Minister of Trade and Industry for the development of the semiconductor industry and semiconductor clusters, including projects to promote the establishment and attraction of advanced semiconductor enterprises.
(3) The State and local governments may provide necessary support, including subsidies, to business operators engaged in the semiconductor industry and enterprises located in semiconductor clusters, as prescribed by relevant statutes and regulations, including Article 14-2 of the Foreign Investment Promotion Act, Article 12 of the Act on Assistance to Korean Off-Shore Enterprises in Repatriation, and Article 86 of the Special Act on Local Decentralization and Balanced Growth. <Amended on June 2, 2026>
(4) Notwithstanding the State Property Act, the Public Property and Commodity Management Act, or any other Act, the State and local governments may reduce or exempt usage fees and rents for State-owned property and public property, including land, owned by the State or local governments for business operators engaged in the semiconductor industry and enterprises [referring to foreign-invested enterprises under Article 2 (1) 6 of the Foreign Investment Promotion Act (hereinafter referred to as "foreign-invested enterprises") and reshoring enterprises under subparagraph 4 of Article 2 of the Act on Assistance to Korean Off-Shore Enterprises in Repatriation (hereinafter referred to as "reshoring enterprises"), but excluding enterprises located in the Seoul metropolitan area as defined in subparagraph 1 of Article 2 of the Seoul Metropolitan Area Readjustment Planning Act], institutions, and knowledge industry centers located in semiconductor clusters. In such cases, usage fees and rents for State-owned property and the rates of reduction or exemption shall be as prescribed by Presidential Decree, and detailed matters concerning public property, including projects eligible for reductions or exemptions of usage fees and rents, the usage fees and rents, and the rates of reduction or exemption, shall be prescribed by ordinance of the relevant local government.
(5) Where necessary to promote semiconductor investment, the State and local governments may reduce or exempt the following charges for business operators engaged in the semiconductor industry and industrial cluster infrastructure, enterprises, institutions, and knowledge industry centers installed or located in semiconductor clusters:
1. Charges for creating replacement forest resources under Article 19 of the Mountainous Districts Management Act;
2. Farmland preservation charges under Article 38 of the Farmland Act;
3. Charges for creating replacement grassland under Article 23 (8) of the Grassland Act;
4. Traffic inducement charges under Article 36 of the Urban Traffic Improvement Promotion Act.
(6) The State and local governments may purchase land designated as semiconductor clusters and lease it. In such cases, matters concerning site purchases, including the requirements for such purchases, shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.
(7) To promote semiconductor investment, the State and local governments may reduce or exempt national and local taxes for foreign-invested enterprises and reshoring enterprises prescribed by Presidential Decree that are engaged in the semiconductor industry or located in semiconductor clusters, as prescribed by the Restriction of Special Taxation Act, the Customs Act, and the Restriction of Special Local Taxation Act.
(8) The State and local governments may provide funds necessary for the development of sites to be leased to enterprises falling under any of the following subparagraphs and for reductions or exemptions of rents for land, etc., and may provide enterprises falling under subparagraph 1 or 2 with funds necessary for the installation and operation of facilities related to attracting enterprises and investment, including medical facilities, educational facilities, research facilities, and housing:
1. Foreign-invested enterprises;
2. Reshoring enterprises;
3. Enterprises investing in semiconductor technology and semiconductor products;
4. Enterprises investing in the semiconductor industry;
5. Enterprises located in semiconductor clusters.
(9) Where local governments provide funds under paragraph (8), the State shall support them as prescribed by Presidential Decree.
(10) Any of the following institutions may provide credit guarantees on a priority basis to enable business operators engaged in the semiconductor industry and enterprises located in semiconductor clusters to smoothly raise the funds they need:
1. The Korea Credit Guarantee Fund under the Korea Credit Guarantee Fund Act;
2. The Korea Technology Finance Corporation under the Korea Technology Finance Corporation Act;
3. Credit guarantee foundations established under Article 9 of the Regional Credit Guarantee Foundation Act.

CHAPTER IV SUPPORT FOR THE SEMICONDUCTOR INDUSTRY, ETC.

Article 16 (Implementation of Technology Development Projects for the Semiconductor Industry, etc.) The Government may implement the following technology development projects to strengthen the competitiveness of the semiconductor industry:
1. Research and development projects to close technology gaps with other countries;
2. Projects to establish centers for performance verification and demonstration for technology development in the semiconductor industry;
3. Other technology development projects that the Minister of Trade and Industry deems necessary to strengthen the competitiveness of the domestic semiconductor industry.

Article 17 (Support for Innovative Development of Small and Medium Enterprises, etc.) The Government may provide support for the following matters for the innovative development of small and medium enterprises or middle-standing enterprises engaged in the semiconductor industry:
1. Research and development, demonstration, safety management, and the establishment of related infrastructure;
2. Support for specialized personnel for research and development or the operation of research equipment, and the development and operation of educational programs for training personnel;
3. Preferential recommendations for the selection of designated military service enterprises in which technical research personnel under Article 36 of the Military Service Act are to serve;
4. Support for employment subsidies for specialized researchers in science and engineering (limited to persons who have obtained an academic degree at or above the level prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Trade and Industry);
5. Support for attracting outstanding personnel from overseas;
6. Support for technology protection and responses to intellectual property disputes;
7. Guidance and advice on strategies for overseas expansion, including overseas patent applications;
8. Other support prescribed by Presidential Decree as necessary to create an ecosystem for the development of the semiconductor industry.

Article 18 (Installation and Expansion of Industrial Infrastructure for Development of the Semiconductor Industry)
(1) To ensure the smooth provision of industrial infrastructure for the development of the semiconductor industry (including redundant facilities in preparation for natural disasters or other incidents), the Government shall incorporate matters necessary for the installation and expansion of power grids, water supply networks, and road networks into the basic plan for electricity supply and demand under Article 25 of the Electric Utility Act, the national basic waterworks plan under Article 4 of the Water Supply and Waterworks Installation Act, and the road construction and management plan under Article 6 of the Road Act, respectively.
(2) Matters necessary for the installation and expansion of industrial infrastructure under paragraph (1) shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.

Article 19 (Support for the Semiconductor Contract Manufacturing Industry, etc.)
(1) To strengthen the competitiveness of the semiconductor industry, the Government shall formulate and implement development policies for the industries falling under items (b) and (c) of subparagraph 1 of Article 2.
(2) Specific matters concerning the industries eligible for the development policies under paragraph (1), the details of support, etc. shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.

Article 20 (Financial Support for Employment, etc.) The State and local governments may provide administrative and financial support necessary for employment to facilitate the securing of personnel for the semiconductor industry.

Article 21 (Selection of Designated Military Service Enterprises, etc.) To strengthen the competitiveness of the semiconductor industry and create an innovation ecosystem, the Government shall endeavor to provide support, including assignment as skilled industrial personnel, for persons employed by designated military service enterprises, etc. related to the semiconductor industry under Article 36 of the Military Service Act, in accordance with the criteria prescribed by that Act and other relevant statutes and regulations.

Article 22 (Support for Approval of and Reporting on Exports of Semiconductor Industry Technologies, etc.) Where necessary in connection with approval of or reporting on exports of national core technologies in the semiconductor industry under Article 11 of the Act on Prevention of Divulgence and Protection of Industrial Technology, the Minister of Trade and Industry may simplify the relevant procedures following deliberation and resolution by the Committee.

Article 23 (Applications for Regulatory Improvements, etc.)
(1) Enterprises, institutions, and knowledge industry centers located in semiconductor clusters may apply to the Minister of Trade and Industry for regulatory improvements where necessary to achieve objectives related to research and development, testing and evaluation, verification, and production activities in the advanced semiconductor industry.
(2) Upon receiving an application under paragraph (1), the Minister of Trade and Industry shall notify the heads of relevant administrative agencies of the details of the application, and the heads of the relevant administrative agencies shall review those details and provide the Minister of Trade and Industry with a written response on the results within 30 days from the date of receipt of the notice. In such cases, the heads of relevant administrative agencies shall proactively handle matters in the application that do not require the revision of statutes and regulations.
(3) Where the head of a relevant administrative agency requires a person who has applied for regulatory improvements under paragraph (1) to supplement data in order to review whether to improve the regulations, the period spent supplementing the relevant data shall not be included in the response period under paragraph (2). In such cases, a response on the results of the review shall be provided within 45 days from the date of receipt of the notice, and where a response cannot be provided, an extension of the response period may be requested only once for a period not exceeding 30 days.
(4) Upon receiving an application under paragraph (1), the Minister of Trade and Industry shall request deliberation by the Committee, and the Committee shall deliberate on the details of the application under paragraph (1), the details of the review by relevant administrative agencies, the results of handling the application, whether to improve the regulations, etc. In such cases, the Committee or the Minister of Trade and Industry may request the heads of relevant administrative agencies to attend a meeting of the Committee.
(5) The Minister of Trade and Industry may notify the heads of relevant administrative agencies of the results of the Committee's deliberation under paragraph (4) and request regulatory improvements, and the heads of relevant administrative agencies may improve the relevant regulations with due regard for the results of the Committee's deliberation.
(6) Where it is necessary to revise statutes and regulations in light of the results of the Committee's deliberation under paragraph (4), the heads of relevant administrative agencies shall review whether such revision is necessary and expeditiously pursue the revision of the relevant statutes and regulations. In such cases, where the Committee deems it necessary, including where two or more heads of relevant administrative agencies are involved, regulatory improvements may be requested from the Regulatory Reform Committee under Article 23 of the Framework Act on Administrative Regulations.
(7) Where, as a result of the Committee's deliberation under paragraph (4), special regulatory exceptions are deemed necessary for demonstration activities, including research and development, testing and evaluation, verification, and production activities in the semiconductor field, exceptions to the relevant regulations may be granted in accordance with the procedures under Article 10-3 of the Industrial Convergence Promotion Act, Article 38-2 of the Special Act on Promotion of Information and Communications Technology, Vitalization of Convergence Thereof, etc., and Article 86 of the Act on Special Cases Concerning the Regulation of Regulation-Free Special Zones and Special Economic Zones for Specialized Regional Development. Provided, That, with respect to exceptions under Article 86 of the Act on Special Cases Concerning the Regulation of Regulation-Free Special Zones and Special Economic Zones for Specialized Regional Development, the competent Mayors/Do Governors shall be notified of the results of the Committee's deliberation, and the Mayors/Do Governors may review those results and apply to the Minister of SMEs and Startups concerning matters related to special regulatory exceptions pursuant to Articles 72 and 81 of that Act.
(8) Details concerning paragraphs (1) through (7), and matters necessary concerning the criteria, procedures, and methods for reviewing regulatory improvements, shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.

Article 24 (Special Cases Concerning Preliminary Feasibility Studies)
(1) To ensure national and economic security in relation to the semiconductor industry, secure stable supply chains, rapidly strengthen future competitiveness, etc., the Minister of Planning and Budget and the Minister of Science and ICT may, following deliberation and resolution by the Committee, give priority to selecting the following projects as projects subject to preliminary feasibility studies under Article 38 of the National Finance Act and relevant statutes and regulations and Article 12-3 of the Framework Act on Science and Technology:
1. Projects to develop industrial infrastructure for semiconductor clusters under Article 14;
2. Projects to support enterprises and institutions located in semiconductor clusters, etc. under Article 15;
3. Technology development projects for the semiconductor industry under Article 16;
4. Other projects for which the Committee recognizes that specific project plans, including project purposes, scale, and implementation methods, have been established to strengthen the competitiveness of the semiconductor industry and promote its innovative growth.
(2) The Minister of Planning and Budget and the Minister of Science and ICT shall endeavor to ensure that preliminary feasibility studies are conducted expeditiously for projects selected as projects subject to preliminary feasibility studies under paragraph (1).
(3) Notwithstanding Article 38 (1) of the National Finance Act, the Minister of Planning and Budget or the Minister of Science and ICT may exempt from a preliminary feasibility study a project specified in any subparagraph of paragraph (1) that is recognized as needing to be implemented particularly expeditiously to ensure national and economic security, secure stable supply chains, secure future competitiveness, etc. In such cases, the Minister of Planning and Budget and the Minister of Science and ICT shall, without delay, report the details of the projects exempted from preliminary feasibility studies and the grounds for exemption to the competent standing committee of the National Assembly under Article 5 (4).
(4) Specific matters, including eligible projects and requirements under paragraph (1), shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.

Article 25 (Special Cases Concerning Preliminary Feasibility Studies for Projects of Public Enterprises and Quasi-Governmental Institutions)
(1) Where a project specified in any subparagraph of Article 24 (1) has, following deliberation and resolution by the Committee, been recognized as needing to be implemented particularly expeditiously to ensure national and economic security, secure stable industrial supply chains, secure future competitiveness, etc., the head of a public enterprise or quasi-governmental institution (hereinafter referred to as the "head of an institution" in this Article) may apply to the Minister of Finance and Economy for exemption from a preliminary feasibility study, notwithstanding the main clause of Article 40 (3) of the Act on the Management of Public Institutions.
(2) Upon receiving an application for exemption from a preliminary feasibility study under paragraph (1), the Minister of Finance and Economy shall, after seeking advice from relevant experts, decide whether to grant the exemption and notify the head of the institution of the decision.
(3) Where necessary to ensure the financial soundness of public enterprises and quasi-governmental institutions, the efficient implementation of projects, etc., the Minister of Finance and Economy may require the head of an institution to review the appropriateness of the project plan for a project granted exemption from a preliminary feasibility study under paragraph (2), including financing plans, total project costs, and analysis of efficient alternatives.
(4) Other matters necessary concerning the procedures, etc. for preliminary feasibility studies for projects of public enterprises and quasi-governmental institutions shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.

Article 26 (Deemed Authorizations and Permissions, etc. under Other Acts) Where the Minister of Trade and Industry approves a semiconductor cluster development plan or modifies such approval pursuant to Article 11 (5), any of the following permissions, decisions, authorizations, licenses, consultations, consents, approvals, releases, or dispositions, etc. (hereinafter referred to as "authorizations and permissions, etc.") concerning matters on which the Minister of Trade and Industry has consulted in advance with the heads of relevant administrative agencies shall be deemed to have been obtained, and where public notice is given of the approval or modification of approval of a semiconductor cluster development plan, public notice of the authorizations and permissions, etc. under the Acts specified in the following subparagraphs shall be deemed to have been given:
1. Matters for which authorizations and permissions, etc. are deemed to have been obtained pursuant to Article 21 of the Industrial Sites and Development Act;
2. Matters for which authorizations and permissions, etc. are deemed to have been obtained pursuant to Article 13-2 of the Industrial Cluster Development and Factory Establishment Act;
3. Permission to occupy and use a road under Article 18 of the Act on the Maintenance and Improvement of Road Networks in Agricultural and Fishing Villages;
4. An impact assessment under subparagraph 2 of Article 9 (1) of the National Heritage Impact Assessment Act.

Article 27 (Special Cases Concerning Expedited Processing of Authorizations and Permissions, etc.)
(1) Where a project implementer of a semiconductor cluster develops the site of the semiconductor cluster, or the State, local governments, etc. develop industrial infrastructure for the semiconductor cluster under Article 14 (1) for its smooth operation, and delays in authorizations and permissions, etc. under the subparagraphs of Article 26 are likely to significantly impede the development or operation of the semiconductor cluster, an application may be made to the Minister of Trade and Industry for expedited processing of the relevant authorizations and permissions, etc.
(2) Upon receiving an application under paragraph (1), the Minister of Trade and Industry may, following deliberation and resolution by the Committee, request expedited processing of the relevant authorizations and permissions, etc. from the head of the administrative agency vested with authority over them (hereinafter referred to as the "permitting authority"). In such cases, the matter shall be referred to the Committee for deliberation after hearing the opinion of the relevant permitting authority, and the permitting authority shall process the authorizations and permissions, etc. without delay unless there are special grounds prescribed by Presidential Decree.
(3) A permitting authority receiving a request under paragraph (2) shall respond to the Minister of Trade and Industry with a processing plan for the authorizations and permissions, etc. within 15 days, and where the permitting authority requires the project implementer to supplement data in order to formulate the processing plan, the period spent supplementing the relevant data shall not be included in the response period. Provided, That, even in such cases, the processing plan shall be provided within 30 days.
(4) A permitting authority that has submitted a processing plan under paragraph (3) shall notify the project implementer of the results of processing the authorizations and permissions, etc. within 15 days from the date of submission. Provided, That, where unavoidable grounds for an extension arise in the course of processing the authorizations and permissions, etc., an extension may be requested only once for a period not exceeding 15 days.
(5) Where a permitting authority fails to respond to the Minister of Trade and Industry with a processing plan for the authorizations and permissions, etc. within the processing period under paragraph (3), or fails to notify the project implementer of the results of processing the authorizations and permissions, etc. within the processing period under paragraph (4), the processing of the authorizations and permissions, etc. shall be deemed completed on the day on which 60 days have elapsed from the date of the request under paragraph (2).
(6) Other matters necessary for deliberation and resolution on expedited processing of authorizations and permissions, etc. shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.

Article 28 (Special Cases concerning Location)
When allocating the total allowable volume of factory construction under Article 18 (2) of the Seoul Metropolitan Area Readjustment Planning Act, Mayors/Do Governors shall give priority consideration to enterprises engaged in the semiconductor industry.

Article 29 (Special Cases concerning Development of Semiconductor Materials, Components, and Equipment Industries and System Semiconductor Industry and Promotion of Supply Chain Localization)
(1) The Government may implement the following measures to promote the development of the domestic semiconductor materials, components, and equipment industries and the system semiconductor industry and the localization of supply chains (meaning all activities carried out to reduce dependence on overseas imports or on particular countries in supply chains under subparagraph 2 of Article 2 of the Framework Act on Supporting Supply Chain Stabilization for Economic Security; hereinafter the same shall apply):
1. Priority funding for research and development undertaken jointly by domestic materials, components, and equipment enterprises and system semiconductor enterprises in cooperation with domestic user enterprises;
2. Designation and operation of test beds (meaning the infrastructure of facilities and equipment necessary for demonstration tests, reliability assessments, performance verification, etc.) to confirm the performance of products developed by domestic materials, components, and equipment enterprises and system semiconductor enterprises;
3. Establishment of advanced research and development complexes for materials, components, and equipment, convergence research and development complexes for system semiconductors, etc. within semiconductor clusters, allocation of sites therefor, and reduction of or exemption from rent for State-owned and publicly owned land, to attract research and development personnel to domestic materials, components, and equipment enterprises and system semiconductor enterprises.
(2) The methods and procedures for implementing the measures specified in the subparagraphs of paragraph (1) and other necessary matters shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.

Article 30 (Special Cases concerning Identification and Attraction of Highly Qualified Overseas Personnel)
(1) The Government may conduct investigations and analyses of specialized personnel at overseas universities, research institutions, and enterprises to identify and attract highly qualified overseas personnel for the semiconductor industry.
(2) The Minister of Trade and Industry may use the results of the investigations and analyses under paragraph (1), as prescribed by Presidential Decree, to make use of information on highly qualified overseas personnel.
(3) The Minister of Trade and Industry may carry out projects relating to the establishment of international networks, participation in international events, etc. to attract highly qualified overseas personnel for the semiconductor industry.
(4) Notwithstanding Articles 8, 10, 10-2, and 10-3 of the Immigration Act, the Minister of Justice may, in consultation with the Minister of Trade and Industry or the Minister of Science and ICT, prescribe different criteria and procedures for issuing visas to foreigners engaged in the semiconductor industry and different maximum periods of stay that may be granted at one time for each status of stay.
(5) The Government may provide administrative and financial support necessary to identify and attract highly qualified overseas personnel.
(6) Matters necessary for the identification and attraction of highly qualified overseas personnel and the administrative and financial support under paragraph (5) shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.

Article 31 (Tax Support for Enterprises Related to Semiconductor Industry)
The State and local governments may provide enterprises related to the semiconductor industry with various forms of tax support, including tax reductions and exemptions, in accordance with the criteria prescribed in relevant tax statutes, such as the Restriction of Special Taxation Act and the Restriction of Special Local Taxation Act, to strengthen the competitiveness of the semiconductor industry and create an innovation ecosystem.

CHAPTER V SUPPORT FOR TRAINING OF SEMICONDUCTOR INDUSTRY PERSONNEL

Article 32 (Training and Securing of Semiconductor Industry Personnel)
(1) The Government shall actively endeavor to secure personnel for semiconductor design, research and development, manufacturing, etc. for the development of the semiconductor industry.
(2) The Government shall pursue the following matters to secure highly qualified personnel for the semiconductor industry:
1. Matters concerning improvement of the professional competencies of semiconductor industry personnel and support therefor;
2. Matters concerning research and development, education, and support for semiconductor industry personnel;
3. Matters concerning vocational training and the development and improvement of vocational skills;
4. Matters concerning employment retention and stability, including promotion of the influx of new personnel;
5. Matters concerning the use of skills and knowledge possessed by skilled semiconductor personnel, such as retired workers, the establishment of databases (meaning collections of information systematically organized and processed to enable users to search and use it), and the passing on of technical skills;
6. Matters concerning the promotion of welfare, including improvement of workplace environments for semiconductor processes and improvement of the treatment of workers;
7. Matters concerning support for attracting highly qualified overseas personnel;
8. Relocation of four-year universities located in Seoul and related to semiconductor technology to semiconductor clusters;
9. Other matters prescribed by Presidential Decree to secure semiconductor industry personnel.

Article 33 (Designation of Institutions for Training Specialized Semiconductor Industry Personnel)
(1) The Minister of Trade and Industry may designate institutions, organizations, etc. meeting criteria prescribed by Presidential Decree as institutions for training specialized semiconductor industry personnel to train personnel necessary for the development of the semiconductor industry.
(2) The Government may provide institutions designated under paragraph (1) with support for the expenses necessary to implement projects for training specialized semiconductor industry personnel.
(3) Matters necessary for the requirements and procedures for designation, support, etc. under paragraphs (1) and (2) shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.

Article 34 (Designation of Universities Specializing in Semiconductors, etc.)
(1) The Government may designate institutions falling under the following subparagraphs as universities specializing in semiconductors, graduate schools specializing in semiconductors, or high schools tailored to semiconductor industry needs (hereinafter referred to as "semiconductor-specialized universities, etc."), in accordance with criteria prescribed by Presidential Decree, to create an excellent semiconductor education environment and develop personnel:
1. Schools under Article 2 of the Higher Education Act, graduate schools under Article 29 of that Act, and graduate school universities under Article 30 of that Act;
2. The Korea Advanced Institute of Science and Technology under the Korea Advanced Institute of Science and Technology Act;
3. The Gwangju Institute of Science and Technology under the Gwangju Institute of Science and Technology Act;
4. The Daegu Gyeongbuk Institute of Science and Technology under the Daegu Gyeongbuk Institute of Science and Technology Act;
5. The Ulsan National Institute of Science and Technology under the Ulsan National Institute of Science and Technology Act;
6. High schools that are schools under Article 2 of the Elementary and Secondary Education Act and operate customized curricula directly linked to industry needs;
7. Universities relocated to semiconductor clusters under Article 32 (2) 8.
(2) The Government may provide support for expenses, etc. necessary for the operation, research and development, etc. of semiconductor-specialized universities, etc. designated under paragraph (1).
(3) The Government may support all or part of the expenses necessary to establish and operate departments and divisions established by contracts under Article 8 of the Industrial Education Enhancement and Industry-Academia-Research Cooperation Promotion Act with business operators engaged in the semiconductor industry, to train personnel tailored to the needs of enterprises in each segment of the semiconductor industry.
(4) Matters necessary for the requirements and procedures for designation, support, etc. under paragraphs (1) and (2) shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.

CHAPTER VI SPECIAL ACCOUNT FOR STRENGTHENING SEMICONDUCTOR INDUSTRY COMPETITIVENESS

Article 35 (Establishment, Operation, and Management of Special Account for Strengthening Semiconductor Industry Competitiveness)
(1) A Special Account for Strengthening Semiconductor Industry Competitiveness (hereinafter referred to as the "Special Account") shall be established to secure financial resources for stable support for strengthening the competitiveness of the semiconductor industry and to implement related projects efficiently.
(2) The Special Account shall be operated and managed by the Minister of Trade and Industry.
(3) Other matters necessary for the establishment, operation, and management of the Special Account shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.

Article 36 (Revenue and Expenditure of Special Account)
(1) The revenue of the Special Account shall consist of the following:
1. Transfers from the general account;
2. Transfers and funds received on deposit from other special accounts or funds;
3. Technology royalties collected from semiconductor-related projects under the Industrial Technology Innovation Promotion Act;
4. Funds received on deposit from the Public Capital Management Fund under the Public Capital Management Fund Act;
5. Income from the sale or management of assets held by the Special Account;
6. Income from principal and interest on funds placed on deposit under the Special Account;
7. Loan repayments, interest income, and other receipts from Special Account funds;
8. Borrowings;
9. Donations of money or goods from individuals, corporations, or organizations;
10. Receipts other than those specified in subparagraphs 1 through 9.
(2) The expenditure of the Special Account shall cover the following:
1. Surveys of the current state of the semiconductor industry, forecasts of its development prospects, and compilation of statistics;
2. Expenses necessary for designation, operation, management, etc. of semiconductor clusters;
3. Support for the development and operation of industrial infrastructure in semiconductor clusters;
4. Projects for implementing policies to foster semiconductor clusters;
5. Support for enterprises, institutions, etc. located in semiconductor clusters;
6. Promotion of technology development projects, etc. for the semiconductor industry;
7. Support for the innovative development of small and medium enterprises, etc. engaged in the semiconductor industry;
8. Support for the semiconductor contract manufacturing industry, etc.;
9. Support for employment by enterprises engaged in the semiconductor industry;
10. Projects to develop the semiconductor materials, components, and equipment industries and the system semiconductor industry and promote supply chain localization;
11. Support for identifying and attracting highly qualified overseas personnel;
12. Projects to train and secure semiconductor industry personnel and the establishment and operation of institutions for training specialized personnel;
13. Expenses for the establishment, operation, and management of the Special Account;
14. Repayment of principal and interest on borrowings and funds received on deposit;
15. Other matters prescribed by Presidential Decree as necessary for strengthening the competitiveness of the semiconductor industry.

Article 37 (Transfers from General Account, Other Special Accounts, and Funds, etc.)
(1) Where the revenue of the Special Account is insufficient to cover all expenses chargeable to the Special Account, or in similar cases, all or part of the shortfall may be covered by transfers from the general account, other special accounts, or funds.
(2) A reserve fund may be appropriated in the expenditure budget of the Special Account to cover unforeseeable expenditure not provided for in the budget or expenditure exceeding the budget.
(3) Temporary borrowings may be made when the Special Account temporarily lacks funds. In such cases, amounts necessary for repayment of the borrowings, etc. may be transferred from the general account in the budget for the following fiscal year.

Article 38 (Carryover of Budget, etc.)
(1) Notwithstanding Article 48 (1) of the National Finance Act, portions of the expenditure budget of the Special Account that have not been disbursed within the relevant fiscal year due to unavoidable circumstances may be carried over for use in the following year, within the total expenditure budget of the Special Account for each responsible ministry, as prescribed by Presidential Decree: Provided, That such amounts may not be carried over for more than two fiscal years from that fiscal year.
(2) When carrying over an expenditure budget under paragraph (1), the Minister of Trade and Industry shall carry it over for use on the basis of the amounts as at December 31 of the relevant year, prepare a statement of carryover, and send it to the Minister of Finance and Economy, the Minister of Planning and Budget, and the Board of Audit and Inspection by January 31 of the following year.
(3) Any surplus arising from the settlement of accounts of the Special Account shall be transferred to revenue for the following year.

Article 39 (Entrustment of Accounting Affairs)
(1) The Minister of Trade and Industry may entrust part of the affairs under Article 36 to a bank under the Banking Act or to a corporation prescribed by Presidential Decree, as prescribed by Presidential Decree.
(2) Where affairs are entrusted under paragraph (1), the Minister of Trade and Industry may pay handling fees and other necessary expenses, as prescribed by Presidential Decree.
(3) Where affairs are entrusted under paragraph (1), the Minister of Trade and Industry may appoint accounting personnel to perform the relevant affairs from among the officers and employees of the corporation entrusted with such affairs.
(4) The Act on Liability of Accounting Personnel, etc. shall apply mutatis mutandis to accounting personnel appointed under paragraph (3).

Article 40 (Support through Other Special Accounts)
Where the Government deems it more effective to carry out projects through a special account falling under any of the following subparagraphs, it may operate projects to strengthen competitiveness, such as support for the development and operation of infrastructure and production facilities for the semiconductor industry and support for talent development, through that special account. <Amended on June 2, 2026>
1. The Special Account for Strengthening the Competitiveness of the Materials, Components, and Equipment Industries and Stabilizing Their Supply Chains under Article 68 of the Act on Special Measures for Strengthening the Competitiveness of the Materials, Components, and Equipment Industries and Stabilizing Their Supply Chains;
2. The Special Account for Balanced Regional Development under Article 74 of the Special Act on Local Decentralization and Balanced Growth;
3. Other special accounts prescribed by Presidential Decree.

CHAPTER VII SUPPLEMENTARY PROVISIONS, ETC.

Article 41 (Submission of Materials and Inspections, etc.)
(1) Where necessary for supervision, the Minister of Trade and Industry or the head of a relevant administrative agency may order semiconductor cluster management institutions and institutions, corporations, or organizations related to the semiconductor industry to report on their affairs or submit materials concerning such affairs.
(2) The Minister of Trade and Industry or the head of a relevant administrative agency may take necessary measures, such as having public officials under his or her jurisdiction enter the offices, business establishments, and other premises of the semiconductor cluster management institutions and the institutions, corporations, or organizations related to the semiconductor industry under paragraph (1) to inspect relevant documents or question relevant persons.
(3) Public officials entering premises or conducting inspections under paragraph (2) shall carry identification indicating their authority and present it to the relevant persons.

Article 42 (Delegation and Entrustment of Powers, etc.)
(1) Part of the powers of the Minister of Trade and Industry under this Act may be delegated to Mayors/Do Governors or the heads of Sis/Guns/Gus (in the case of Gus, referring to autonomous Gus), as prescribed by Presidential Decree.
(2) Part of the duties of the Minister of Trade and Industry or Mayors/Do Governors under this Act may be entrusted to relevant institutions or corporations, as prescribed by Presidential Decree.

Article 43 (Special Cases concerning Exemption from Liability for Proactive Administration)
No liability, such as a request for disciplinary action or censure under the Board of Audit and Inspection Act or the Act on Public Sector Audits, shall be imposed for the results of proactively performing tasks under Articles 23, 26, and 27 in the absence of willful misconduct or gross negligence in the acts concerned.

Article 44 (Administrative Fines)
(1) A person who fails to submit relevant materials or submits false materials in violation of Article 41 (1), or who refuses, evades, or obstructs entry, inspection, etc. under paragraph (2) of that Article, shall be subject to an administrative fine not exceeding 10 million won.
(2) The administrative fines under paragraph (1) shall be imposed and collected by the Minister of Trade and Industry, as prescribed by Presidential Decree.

